1441   // environment with information about the irq/debug_req/nmi state that applies to a particular
1442   // instruction.
1443   //
1444   // When a irq/debug_req/nmi appears the ID stage will finish whatever instruction it is currently
1445   // executing (if any) then take the trap the cycle after that instruction leaves the ID stage. The
1446   // trap taken depends upon the state of irq/debug_req/nmi on that cycle. In the cycles following
1447   // that before the first instruction of the trap handler enters the ID stage the state of
1448   // irq/debug_req/nmi could change but this has no effect on the trap handler (e.g. a higher
1449   // priority interrupt might appear but this wouldn't stop the lower priority interrupt trap
1450   // handler executing first as it's already being fetched). To provide the DV environment with the
1451   // correct information for it to verify execution we need to capture the irq/debug_req/nmi state
1452   // the cycle the trap decision is made. Which the captured_X signals below do.
1453   //
1454   // The new_X signals take the raw irq/debug_req/nmi inputs and factor in the enable terms required
1455   // to determine if a trap will actually happen.
